            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"Protection, refuge, and the comforting presence of God, often depicted as the shadow of wings or a great rock offering shelter from the heat.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Psalm 17:8\",\"Psalm 36:7\",\"Psalm 91:1\",\"Isaiah 25:4\",\"Isaiah 32:2\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"A typological prefiguration or incomplete representation of a greater spiritual reality, specifically the Old Covenant pointing to Christ.\",\"basis\":[\"Colossians 2:17\",\"Hebrews 8:5\",\"Hebrews 10:1\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"The brevity, fragility, and fleeting nature of human life.\",\"basis\":[\"Job 8:9\",\"Job 14:2\",\"Psalm 102:11\",\"Psalm 144:4\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Deep gloom, spiritual darkness, or the realm of death (e.g., the valley of the shadow of death).\",\"basis\":[\"Psalm 23:4\",\"Isaiah 9:2\",\"Luke 1:79\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"The symbolic meaning of a shadow is highly context-dependent in Scripture. In the Old Testament, it primarily contrasts the heat of the sun (danger, judgment) with shade (divine protection), or illustrates how quickly human life passes. In the New Testament, it serves a critical theological function distinguishing the anticipatory shadows of the law from the substantial reality of Christ.\"}",
